{"chapter": "topologies", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "020L", "placeholders": {"REF_0001": "\\cite{SGA1}", "REF_0002": "\\cite{Ner}", "REF_0003": "\\ref{sites-definition-site}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:512d6aba66c504dd6441cb0c5d1403481b812af4f0149cb0face59deebfe2184", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nIn this document we explain what the different topologies on the\ncategory of schemes are. Some references are <REF_0001> and <REF_0002>.\nBefore doing so we would like to point out that there are many\ndifferent choices of sites (as defined in\nSites, Definition <REF_0003>) which give rise to\nthe same notion of sheaf on the underlying category. Hence\nour choices may be slightly different from those in the references\nbut ultimately lead to the same cohomology groups, etc.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:cec44a64f325012eef2f5b8a1a5fc1a569d37d927145cc553366b6a4dccde986", "unit_id": "tag:020L:prose-0001"}
